 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the public service law, in relation to certain requirements\nregarding billing for electric services",
    "basePrintNo": "S7708",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7708-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§66, 44 & 38, add §54, Pub Serv L",
    "lawSection": "Public Service Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S7708\n\nSPONSOR: JACKSON\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the public service law, in relation to certain require-\nments regarding billing for electric services\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to provide pricing transparency of electric\nbills for residents and businesses, and to provide customers with better\npredictability of future electric bills.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 adds a new subdivision 29 to Section 66 of the Public Service\nLaw provides that the manner and method in which customers are billed\nfor their electrical use.\n\nSection 2 amends subdivision 14 of Section 66 of the Public Service Law\nto provide that classifications of service must be indicated on custom-\ners' bills for electric service and must include the website address to\nthe specific public service commission leaf.\n\nSection 3 amends subdivision 1 of Section 44 of the Public Service Law\nto provide that detailed line item transparency and peak pricing of that\nitem over the preceding 52-week period.\n\nSection 4 adds a new subdivision 6 to Section 44 of the Public Service\nLaw to provide that customers that currently receive electronic data\ninterchange by protocols approved by the commission shall be exempt from\nreceiving printed bill statements if such customer's aggregate annual\ndemand per year exceeds three megawatts. Additionally, printed billing\nstatements are not required to be sent if both the customer and the\nvendor are state public benefit corporations.\n\nSection 4 also adds a new subdivision 7 to Section 44 of the Public\nService Law to provide a disclosure notice regarding demand delivery\ncharges.\n\nSection 5 adds a new Section 54 to the Public Service Law that provides\nthe manner and method in which residential rental building submetering\nmay be implemented.\n\nSection 6 amends subdivision 3 of Section 38 of the Public Service Law\nto provide that every utility corporation or municipality must notify\nresidential customers within 90 days of the level billing period if the\nutility corporation projects a shortfall at the end of the level billing\nperiod.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThis bill would allow many New Yorkers across the state to fully under-\nstand their electric bill and make informed decisions regarding their\nelectric use and habits. The bill will also provide relief to vendors\nthat provide electricity to clients like the MTA and similar government\nagencies. The bill will provide regulatory protections to residential\ntenants that are submeterered by DPS licensed entities.  The bill would\nprovide level billing customers fair notice that additional payments may\nbe required during a level billing cycle.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nSTATE AND LOCAL FISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nTo be determined.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the one hundred eightieth day after it\nshall have become a law. Effective immediately, the addition, amendment\nand/or repeal of any rule or regulation necessary for the implementation\nof this act on its effective date are authorized to be made and\ncompleted on or before such date.\n",
